CodeRabbit's fourth full pass (on 29a0847a4), update-check findings:
- The permit was released when SIGKILL was requested, not when the provider exited. Fix: the teardown now waits for Node's exit event, bounded to 2 s if the kill never takes effect, so a fifth provider can never start beside four still alive. Unit test: a provider whose kill never takes effect holds its teardown for exactly 2 s.
- Already fixed in 69dc1bc4e and b176167f9: teardown outside the lock's critical path (results never wait for teardown; helpers are bounded to 2 s) and the five-entry queued-lookup test.
- Major, declined with the documented reason: a descendant listed by ps could exit and have its PID reused before the signal. Node exposes no per-descendant handle (pidfd or job object), reuse within that window needs the PID space to wrap, and killProcessTree's doc comment states the limit.
- Docs: a still-running provider is stopped on a best-effort basis; a provider that exited before the deadline has nothing to kill.
- Records: pm-5kfzjh's resolution and contract migrate line state the --fix-managed-state exception. pm-9x9drr's title and actual result describe the remaining issue.
- Tests:
  - the stalled npm fixture records each invocation, and the test asserts two runs;
  - the liveness probe treats only ESRCH as absence;
  - exitWithin clears its timer;
  - fixture pid paths reach the scripts as environment data;
  - an exited launcher's pid is never signalled in cleanup;
  - the root is asserted to be killed after its descendants;
  - the Windows root exits while taskkill runs;
  - the signal from treeRoot.kill() itself is recorded;
  - a failed spawn has no PID and needs no tree teardown;
  - performance.now spies are restored.
